// addresses.hby — where things are in memory.
//
//     use addresses
//
//     int Count = 5
//     address int A = addresses.of Count      where Count is
//     A.value = 6                             what's there: Count is now 6
//     address int Next = A + 1                the next int along in memory
//     A[2]                                    two ints along, like (A + 2).value
//     address short Port = addresses.at 0x0200    a fixed address (a device's register)
//     address int Nowhere = addresses.none    no address (C's NULL)
//     A.is_none   A.number   B - A   A < B
//
// An address can point at plain data: numbers, bool, char, other addresses,
// and structs and arrays of those. `address` on its own points at nothing
// in particular (C's void *). Functions in C can take and give addresses.
//
// Cabby can't check what an address points at before the program runs:
// that is why addresses need this `use` line. When interpreting, Cabby
// checks reading and writing through addresses of its own values (stepping
// past the end of an array is a panic); compiled programs don't check.
//
// These are built into Cabby; this header turns them on. The full
// reference is addresses.md, next to this file.
